tion programmes until financing improves. Pellham is the largest single buyer of grid equipment in Caldria, and its 2027 capex plan had been up 18%. This is the clearest evidence that equity funding is failing.\n- **Sector backdrop:** Two other utility share offerings were cut back in size (Q3 2026 credit conditions report). Equity is therefore only partly available, and on smaller terms.\n- **Norvane Utilities:** Its 2027 capex is up 12%. It priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably, so it is leaning on debt rather than equity.\n- **Debt side:** Utility bond spreads widened 30bp in Q3 to 165bp. Debt is still accessible but costs more.\n\n**Implication:** Capex that depends on new equity, as Pellham's does, will probably be scaled back or deferred. Utilities with debt capacity, like Norvane, are better placed. For grid-equipment demand, the main risk is Pellham's roughly 40% cut to 2027 transformer orders.\n\n**Caveats:** I found only these three sources. I found no data on equity issuance sizes or valuations, or on Norvane's leverage headroom. The conclusion rests mainly on Pellham's withdrawal and the two downsized offerings.","ttft_ms":1499,"type":"result","duration_ms":25146,"uuid":"59b8b38d-1611-4790-88ba-754f17688c38","ttft_stream_ms":1274,"time_to_request_ms":76,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":5,"system_prompt":13,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":13,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":0,"transcript_persist":3,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":6,"init_frame_send":3,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":3,"message_prep":0,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":2,"request_announcements":8,"query_setup":0,"model_gates":0,"tool_schema_build":3,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":3,"request_build":5},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index":0,"time_to_request_loop_lag_ms":26,"time_to_request_cpu_ms":66,"time_to_request_major_faults":0,"process_rss_mib_turn_start":205,"process_anon_mib_turn_start":132,"process_rss_mib_result":20